{"page_content": "Test method GENOTOXIC ENDPOINTS measured/ EU/OECD PRINCIPLE OF THE TEST METHOD guidelinea Mammalian Structural and numerical chromosome aberrations / The test EU: B.23 spermatogonial chromosome identifies substances that induce structural chromosome aberrations in mammalian, usually rodent, spermatogonial cells OECD: 483b aberration test and is, therefore, expected to be predictive of induction of heritable mutations in germ cells. An increase in polyploidy may indicate that a substance has the potential to induce numerical chromosome aberrations, but this test is not optimal to measure numerical aberrations and is not routinely used for that purpose. Accordingly, this test guideline is not designed to measure numerical aberrations. Rodent dominant Structural and numerical chromosome aberrations / The test EU: B.22 lethal test identifies substances that induce dominant lethal effects causing embryonic or foetal death resulting from inherited dominant OECD: 478b lethal mutations induced in germ cells of an exposed parent, usually the male. It is generally accepted that dominant lethals are due to structural and numerical chromosome aberrations. Rats or mice are recommended as the test species. Transgenic rodent Gene mutations and chromosomal rearrangements (the latter EU: none (TGR) somatic and germ cell gene specifically in the plasmid and Spi- assay models) / Since the transgenes are transmitted by the germ cells, they are present in OECD: 488 mutation assays every cell. Therefore, gene mutations and/or chromosomal rearrangements can be detected in virtually all tissues of an animal including specific site of contact tissues and germ cells. Delayed sampling times may need to be considered in order to detect mutations in different stages of spermatogenesis. a For EU guidelines, see Regulation (EC) No 440/2008 (http://eur-", "metadata": {"doc_id": "e0135cdb-380c-412f-b3ef-53272d67c834", "content_type": "Table", "filename": "information_requirements_